    {
      "title": "A stated interest-only rate is an assumption, not a quote",
      "text": "If principal is $200,000 and the explicitly assumed simple annual interest rate is 4%, a full year produces $8,000 before any stated adjustments. Principal withdrawal rights and the guaranteed or declared rate come from the contract. Interest earned can have different tax treatment from the death benefit itself; the later tax lessons address that distinction.",
      "example": "Do not describe the hypothetical three-percent teaching example as an available carrier rate.",
      "topics": [
        "5.3.02"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Proceeds held by an insurer have specific legal terms",
      "text": "Section 16-108 permits an insurer to hold proceeds under the policyholder’s written agreement or, absent that arrangement at maturity, an agreement with beneficiaries. Control, revocation and stated protections from beneficiary creditors follow that agreement and law. The funds need not be separately segregated.",
      "example": "A limitation on beneficiary control is not an unlimited promise that all money is immune from every creditor or legal order.",
      "topics": [
        "5.3.02"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Fixed period and fixed amount answer different questions",
      "text": "Under the expressly simplified no-interest, no-fee assumptions, $120,000 over ten years of monthly payments equals $1,000 per month. Selecting $2,000 per month instead gives sixty payments. Real settlement tables incorporate their applicable interest and terms.",
      "example": "Choosing the period fixes duration; choosing the payment amount leaves the duration dependent on the available proceeds and applicable interest.",
      "topics": [
        "5.3.03",
        "5.3.04"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "A lifetime promise differs from a minimum period",
      "text": "Straight life-only payments stop at the measuring life’s death. If an option instead promises life with ten years certain and the person dies after six complete years of monthly payments, the specified remaining four years are payable to the designated recipient under the guarantee. Living beyond ten years still produces life payments.",
      "example": "A pure ten-year fixed-period option ends after ten years; life with ten years certain continues while the measuring life is alive.",
      "topics": [
        "5.3.05",
        "5.3.06"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Joint-and-survivor is an income form",
      "text": "The surviving covered person receives the continuation percentage or amount in the actual option. With an explicit 75% survivor feature applied to an initial $2,000 monthly payment, continuation is $1,500. A 100% feature instead preserves $2,000 under those assumptions.",
      "example": "This is not the same as a joint first-death policy paying a death benefit or survivorship life paying at the last insured’s death.",
      "topics": [
        "5.3.07"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Use the actual death-claim dates",
      "text": "Under §16-109, payment within thirty days after death receives the interest exception. Proof submitted more than 180 days after death shifts the interest start to the submitted-proof date. Otherwise the statutory general start is death. The minimum rate is tied to death proceeds left with the insurer.",
      "example": "Death on January 1 with proof submitted after 180 days and payment later: use the actual proof-submission date as the interest start, not January 1.",
      "topics": [
        "5.1.12"
      ]
    }
